WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.960
Radio astronomers have captured hydrogen signals from when the universe was billions of years younger, giving a fresh window into its early structure. This is Fascinating Frontiers, episode 184. Astronomers used the Miracat radio telescope to pick up emissions from neutral hydrogen gas at vast distances. The observations reach back to a time when the cosmos was billions of years younger than today. These faint radio waves trace the distribution of gas that

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:57.480
later formed stars and galaxies. The data add to efforts to map how matter clumped together in the early universe. Follow up observations with larger arrays are planned to refine the measurements. The detection of ancient hydrogen with Miracat opens a route to mapping gas flows across cosmic time. By isolating the red shifted 21 centimeter line, astronomers can trace neutral hydrogen that has not yet formed stars.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:19.200
The signals originate from an era when the universe was still assembling its first large structures. These observations complement optical surveys by revealing the raw material that later built galaxies. Next steps include cross matching the radio data with optical catalogs to identify which gas clouds evolved into today's visible systems.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:45.520
Picture a single radio wavelength stretched by the expansion of space until it arrives at Earth stretched to several meters long. That stretch turns the original 21 centimeter emission from hydrogen atoms into a signal we can detect with arrays like Miracat. The atoms themselves sit in vast cold clouds that have never collapsed into stars. Each atom occasionally flipping its electron spin and releasing a photon.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:02:12.620
Over billions of years, those photons travel to us. Their wavelength stretched in exact proportion to the distance they crossed. So a telescope tuned across a range of frequencies can slice the universe into successive shells of time. One surprising number stands out. The same transition that is easy to observe nearby becomes a whisper from the epoch when the first galaxies were only beginning to light up, roughly 10 billion years ago.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:30.560
Yet the precise timing of when that hydrogen began to disappear, ionized by the first stars, remains uncertain, because models differ on how quickly those early sources turned on. We can measure the glow, but we still do not know exactly which objects drove the final clearing of the fog.

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:51.960
Now on a completely different note, the newest desi legacy survey release covers three quarters of the sky and catalogs nearly four billion objects. The public database includes stars, galaxies, supernovae, and gravitational lenses. Researchers compiled the map from multiple imaging campaigns over several years.

00:02:51.960 --> 00:03:10.260
It supplies a reference catalog for future studies of large scale structure. The team expects the data to support ongoing analyses of galaxy evolution and dark energy. While desi gives us the wide view, another long running survey just added fresh layers to its own deep archive.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:33.260
SDSS DR20 adds updated observations from several ongoing projects within the survey. The release incorporates new spectra and imaging that expand coverage of stars and galaxies. Astronomers can now access refined measurements across multiple wavelengths. The data continue a long running effort to build a comprehensive view of the nearby universe.

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:45.320
Further releases are scheduled as additional fields are processed. From mapping the cosmos at large scales, we shift inward to the formation history of our own solar system's innermost planet.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:04:02.680
Researchers at the University of Liege and K.U. Louvin used experimental patrology to simulate Mercury's magma ocean. Their work shows how a graphite crust formed and how the planet's core crystallized. The studies appear in Earth and planetary science letters.

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:12.940
Nature communications and advances in geochemistry and cosmochemistry. Beppy Columbus upcoming orbital phase will provide ground truth data for these models.

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:26.660
The experiments help explain why Mercury's surface composition differs sharply from other terrestrial planets. Understanding how planets hold together leads naturally to new work on how rubble pile asteroids might fall apart.

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:34.540
A new scaling framework links the tensile strength of granular asteroids to the size and shape of their constituent particles.

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:44.740
Laboratory tests and modeling show Benu's surface is roughly 50 times weaker than ground coffee. The findings come from a study published in Nature Communications.

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:52.340
Understanding these mechanical properties aids predictions of how rubble pile asteroids respond to impacts or tidal forces.

00:04:52.340 --> 00:05:04.740
Future missions can use the framework to assess surface stability before landing. While we study distant small bodies, two long serving spacecraft that mapped our own magnetic environment just met their end.

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:19.200
ESA's Samba and Salsa spacecraft completed atmospheric reentry this week after 26 years of service. Samba disintegrated over the Pacific on August 31st at 5.39pm Eastern Daylight Time.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:29.700
Scientists use the events to study how spacecraft materials break apart and interact with the upper atmosphere. The pair had mapped Earth's magnetic environment since 2000.

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:40.220
Their end provides a rare controlled observation of orbital decay. From the end of one Earth orbiting mission, we look ahead to planned infrastructure between Earth and the Moon.

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:52.720
Chinese officials outlined an international satellite network to explore cis lunar space. The constellation would support navigation, communication, and scientific observations between Earth and the Moon.

00:05:52.720 --> 00:06:01.080
Development timelines and partner roles remain under discussion. The project aims to create shared infrastructure for future lunar missions.

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:11.360
Details on launch cadence and orbital architecture are expected in coming months. From future lunar plans, we return to something you can observe in tonight's evening sky.

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:22.440
The planet is moving closer to the Sun from Earth's perspective, revealing phases through small telescopes. Observers can now see the crescent shape that was invisible to the naked eye.

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:32.720
The geometry offers a direct view of Venus's orbital position relative to Earth. Continue tracking will show the crescent narrow further before inferior conjunction.

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:44.580
The changing illumination provides a simple demonstration of planetary phases. From our neighboring planet's quiet display, we move to dramatic star formation driven by galactic collisions.

00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:55.140
Galaxy interactions in the group compress molecular gas and trigger collapse into new stars. The process mirrors conditions thought to be common in the earlier universe.

00:06:55.140 --> 00:07:07.340
Observations link the dense gas clouds directly to sites of active star birth. The findings illustrate how external gravitational forces can drive star formation even in disturbed environments.

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:20.880
Additional infrared and radio data are being analyzed to quantify the efficiency of the process. From galaxies interacting across millions of light years, we zoom in on changes within a single nearby supergiant.

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:32.180
Alamo observations reveal the star's inner atmosphere has grown more uneven since the 2020 dimming event. A smaller companion star may be influencing the pattern of spots and flows.

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:41.500
The data mark the first detailed look at these layers since the brightness drop. Continued monitoring will test whether the asymmetry persists or evolves.

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:53.600
The results bear on models of how red supergiants lose mass before exploding. From one star's changing atmosphere, we broaden to how stars across the galaxy create the elements we are made out of.

00:07:54.080 --> 00:08:04.620
Researchers are tracing how stars synthesize and disperse the elements found in planets and living organisms. Explosions at the end of stellar life spread these materials across galaxies.

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:17.620
The work combines spectroscopic observations with nucleosynthesis models. It narrows the possible pathways for heavy element formation. Further data from upcoming surveys will test the remaining uncertainties.

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:31.420
From cosmic element factories, we turn to a regional effort to shape space policy on Earth. A local space advocacy organization is urging candidates to endorse an international agreement on space activities.

00:08:31.420 --> 00:08:40.340
The treaty concerns coordination of launches and orbital operations. Proponents argue it would strengthen the island's emerging space sector.

00:08:40.900 --> 00:08:48.500
Candidates have been asked to state their positions before the vote. The effort highlights growing interest in space policy at the regional level.

00:08:48.500 --> 00:09:03.700
From policy discussions, we close with two practical NASA updates and a new commercial service. A virtual session on October 2nd at 2 p.m. Eastern Daylight Time will cover the RS-25 engine and future Artemis missions.

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:14.800
The event is designed specifically for blind and low-vision participants. It will run approximately two hours and include audio and descriptions. Registration is open to the public.

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:28.040
The webinar continues NASA's efforts to broaden participation in its programs. The AIMS Fire Department will run propane field simulator exercises at Moffitt Federal Airfield from September 8th to 11th.

00:09:28.580 --> 00:09:43.360
Sessions occur daily between 8 a.m. and 8 p.m. Pacific Daylight Time. The training maintains readiness for airfield emergencies. No impact on normal operations is expected. Similar drills occur periodically to keep crews current.

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:54.100
A German startup called Project S offers orbital tracking and threat detection tools. Its initial focus is on cataloging objects that could endanger active satellites.

00:09:54.940 --> 00:10:06.960
Early users include operators seeking improved situational awareness. The service emerges from stealth with a public website and data portal. Further product details are scheduled for release later this year.

